1
00:00:01,400 --> 00:00:02,520
What if you could copy

2
00:00:02,560 --> 00:00:05,120
the smartest people who ever lived?

3
00:00:05,160 --> 00:00:06,080
Not once.

4
00:00:06,120 --> 00:00:07,600
A thousand times.

5
00:00:07,640 --> 00:00:08,720
A million.

6
00:00:08,760 --> 00:00:10,530
And they never slept.

7
00:00:11,320 --> 00:00:13,560
Every medicine you've ever taken,

8
00:00:13,600 --> 00:00:15,560
every light you've ever switched on,

9
00:00:15,600 --> 00:00:17,840
began in minds like these.

10
00:00:17,880 --> 00:00:19,570
And there were never enough of them.

11
00:00:20,280 --> 00:00:21,890
That's about to change.

12
00:00:22,280 --> 00:00:24,800
Somewhere, at the edge of a quiet town,

13
00:00:24,840 --> 00:00:27,490
someone is building this room right now.

14
00:00:27,640 --> 00:00:29,650
And almost nobody has noticed.

15
00:00:29,880 --> 00:00:31,250
And the strangest part?

16
00:00:31,480 --> 00:00:34,210
Building it may turn out to be the easy bit.

17
00:00:35,660 --> 00:00:37,770
The most surprising thing has been

18
00:00:37,810 --> 00:00:39,920
the lack of public recognition

19
00:00:39,960 --> 00:00:43,710
of how close we are to the end of the exponential.

20
00:00:44,110 --> 00:00:45,030
That's the head of one

21
00:00:45,070 --> 00:00:47,190
of the biggest AI labs.

22
00:00:47,230 --> 00:00:50,200
Most of us picture AI as a better chatbot.

23
00:00:50,350 --> 00:00:52,200
Something that writes your emails.

24
00:00:52,430 --> 00:00:53,350
The people building

25
00:00:53,390 --> 00:00:55,400
it picture something else.

26
00:00:55,900 --> 00:00:58,160
We're increasingly close to what I've called

27
00:00:58,200 --> 00:01:00,310
a country of geniuses in a data center:

28
00:01:00,350 --> 00:01:03,360
a set of AI agents that are more capable

29
00:01:03,400 --> 00:01:05,510
than most humans at most things,

30
00:01:05,550 --> 00:01:09,650
and can coordinate at superhuman speed.

31
00:01:10,050 --> 00:01:12,540
In his essay, he spells it out.

32
00:01:12,610 --> 00:01:14,970
Millions of copies, each smarter

33
00:01:15,010 --> 00:01:17,770
than a Nobel Prize winner in most fields.

34
00:01:17,810 --> 00:01:19,610
Each working ten to a hundred

35
00:01:19,650 --> 00:01:21,340
times faster than you.

36
00:01:21,570 --> 00:01:22,730
He thinks it could arrive

37
00:01:22,770 --> 00:01:24,140
within a few years.

38
00:01:24,370 --> 00:01:26,690
Others think it will take much longer,

39
00:01:26,730 --> 00:01:28,780
or won't look like this at all.

40
00:01:30,330 --> 00:01:33,060
To see why this matters, zoom out.

41
00:01:33,290 --> 00:01:34,900
All the way out.

42
00:01:34,970 --> 00:01:37,090
For almost all of human history,

43
00:01:37,130 --> 00:01:39,250
there was one simple rule.

44
00:01:39,290 --> 00:01:41,970
More people meant more ideas.

45
00:01:42,010 --> 00:01:43,330
Economists looking back

46
00:01:43,370 --> 00:01:45,250
a million years found it:

47
00:01:45,290 --> 00:01:48,290
places with more people invented faster.

48
00:01:48,330 --> 00:01:50,450
Because an idea, once found,

49
00:01:50,490 --> 00:01:52,153
can be used by everyone.

50
00:01:53,130 --> 00:01:54,490
But the best ideas are

51
00:01:54,530 --> 00:01:56,210
getting harder to find.

52
00:01:56,250 --> 00:01:58,260
Keeping chips doubling in power

53
00:01:58,330 --> 00:01:59,810
now takes more than eighteen

54
00:01:59,850 --> 00:02:01,010
times the researchers

55
00:02:01,050 --> 00:02:03,650
it did in the early 1970s.

56
00:02:03,690 --> 00:02:04,690
We have been running out

57
00:02:04,730 --> 00:02:06,980
of geniuses for a long time.

58
00:02:07,850 --> 00:02:10,820
Now imagine that limit simply disappears.

59
00:02:11,050 --> 00:02:12,660
Not more scientists.

60
00:02:12,730 --> 00:02:14,450
As many as we want.

61
00:02:14,490 --> 00:02:15,730
That's the real meaning

62
00:02:15,770 --> 00:02:17,860
of a country of geniuses.

63
00:02:19,410 --> 00:02:22,090
And small pieces of it already work.

64
00:02:22,130 --> 00:02:24,410
In London, a team of scientists

65
00:02:24,450 --> 00:02:27,210
spent about a decade on one question:

66
00:02:27,250 --> 00:02:30,170
how do some superbugs pass their genes,

67
00:02:30,210 --> 00:02:33,260
including drug resistance, to other species?

68
00:02:33,330 --> 00:02:34,860
They had found the answer.

69
00:02:35,090 --> 00:02:37,060
They hadn't published it yet.

70
00:02:37,170 --> 00:02:38,730
Then they gave the same question

71
00:02:38,770 --> 00:02:40,860
to an AI system from Google.

72
00:02:41,170 --> 00:02:42,530
Within two days,

73
00:02:42,570 --> 00:02:44,780
its top idea matched theirs.

74
00:02:45,810 --> 00:02:46,810
In another lab,

75
00:02:46,850 --> 00:02:49,010
an AI system called Robin looked

76
00:02:49,050 --> 00:02:49,930
at a common cause

77
00:02:49,970 --> 00:02:52,250
of blindness in older people.

78
00:02:52,290 --> 00:02:54,170
It suggested an eye drop already

79
00:02:54,210 --> 00:02:56,690
sold in Japan for glaucoma.

80
00:02:56,730 --> 00:02:58,050
So far, it has only been

81
00:02:58,090 --> 00:03:00,090
tested on cells in a dish.

82
00:03:00,130 --> 00:03:02,300
It's a lead, not a cure.

83
00:03:02,370 --> 00:03:03,730
But it was a new lead,

84
00:03:03,770 --> 00:03:05,900
and the idea came from a machine.

85
00:03:06,290 --> 00:03:08,090
Keep that eye drop in mind.

86
00:03:08,130 --> 00:03:09,340
We'll meet it again.

87
00:03:10,290 --> 00:03:11,210
And a piece of this

88
00:03:11,250 --> 00:03:13,330
is already in your pocket.

89
00:03:13,370 --> 00:03:16,250
When OpenAI launched GPT-5,

90
00:03:16,290 --> 00:03:18,090
Sam Altman said talking

91
00:03:18,130 --> 00:03:19,210
to it was like talking

92
00:03:19,250 --> 00:03:22,860
to a "PhD-level expert" in any area.

93
00:03:23,170 --> 00:03:27,140
A thousand PhDs, on call, day and night.

94
00:03:27,250 --> 00:03:29,980
Most of us use them to fix our emails.

95
00:03:31,530 --> 00:03:34,180
So what could a whole country of them do?

96
00:03:34,570 --> 00:03:36,450
Amodei's bet: fifty

97
00:03:36,490 --> 00:03:38,210
to a hundred years of progress

98
00:03:38,250 --> 00:03:39,890
in biology and medicine,

99
00:03:39,930 --> 00:03:41,940
squeezed into five to ten.

100
00:03:42,170 --> 00:03:43,810
He calls it the compressed

101
00:03:43,850 --> 00:03:45,300
twenty-first century.

102
00:03:45,690 --> 00:03:48,290
Demis Hassabis, who shared a Nobel Prize

103
00:03:48,330 --> 00:03:51,460
for AI work in biology, goes further.

104
00:04:01,310 --> 00:04:03,510
These are bets, from people who

105
00:04:03,550 --> 00:04:05,880
build and sell this technology.

106
00:04:05,950 --> 00:04:07,230
Many biologists think

107
00:04:07,270 --> 00:04:08,950
they're far too hopeful.

108
00:04:08,990 --> 00:04:10,790
And they have a point.

109
00:04:10,830 --> 00:04:12,150
Because there's a part of this

110
00:04:12,190 --> 00:04:14,680
story almost nobody talks about.

111
00:04:16,630 --> 00:04:19,360
Thinking was never the only slow step.

112
00:04:19,590 --> 00:04:21,710
A new medicine has to be made.

113
00:04:21,750 --> 00:04:23,550
Then tested on cells.

114
00:04:23,590 --> 00:04:25,070
Then on animals.

115
00:04:25,110 --> 00:04:28,960
Then on people, in stages, for years.

116
00:04:29,410 --> 00:04:32,220
And usually it takes, on average, ten years

117
00:04:32,260 --> 00:04:35,770
to go from understanding a target for a disease

118
00:04:35,810 --> 00:04:38,520
all the way to a drug that's ready for the market.

119
00:04:38,560 --> 00:04:40,670
So it's an enormous amount of time and cost,

120
00:04:40,710 --> 00:04:43,070
billions of dollars, a decade or more.

121
00:04:43,110 --> 00:04:45,370
And most drugs fail along the way.

122
00:04:45,410 --> 00:04:47,760
It's only a kind of 10% success rate.

123
00:04:48,160 --> 00:04:51,250
Look at the first drugs designed with AI.

124
00:04:51,360 --> 00:04:54,440
One, for a disease that scars the lungs,

125
00:04:54,480 --> 00:04:57,370
is now in its final stage of human testing.

126
00:04:57,680 --> 00:04:58,760
More than a hundred are

127
00:04:58,800 --> 00:05:00,570
now being tested in people.

128
00:05:00,880 --> 00:05:03,610
So far, not one has been approved.

129
00:05:04,480 --> 00:05:07,320
The geniuses are not the bottleneck anymore.

130
00:05:07,360 --> 00:05:08,680
The door is.

131
00:05:08,720 --> 00:05:11,610
Amodei puts the question better than anyone.

132
00:05:12,110 --> 00:05:14,320
Let's say that happens. That's the starting gun.

133
00:05:14,360 --> 00:05:16,930
How long does it take to cure all the diseases?

134
00:05:17,330 --> 00:05:19,740
Here's what I can't stop thinking about.

135
00:05:19,890 --> 00:05:22,380
You can't make a cell divide faster.

136
00:05:22,610 --> 00:05:24,290
But almost everything else about

137
00:05:24,330 --> 00:05:26,410
that door was built by people.

138
00:05:26,450 --> 00:05:29,420
Rules, labs, money, priorities.

139
00:05:29,570 --> 00:05:32,620
And what people built, people can change.

140
00:05:34,370 --> 00:05:37,770
So pause here, and imagine it's your call.

141
00:05:37,810 --> 00:05:40,410
The geniuses are coming either way.

142
00:05:40,450 --> 00:05:42,700
Three choices are still open.

143
00:05:42,770 --> 00:05:45,850
How fast we can test their ideas safely.

144
00:05:45,890 --> 00:05:47,770
Who gets the results.

145
00:05:47,810 --> 00:05:49,290
And what we ask them to work

146
00:05:49,330 --> 00:05:50,727
on in the first place.

147
00:05:51,810 --> 00:05:53,180
What would you do?

148
00:05:56,930 --> 00:05:59,820
Let's imagine it's 2035.

149
00:05:59,890 --> 00:06:03,260
This part is a scenario, not a forecast.

150
00:06:03,330 --> 00:06:04,850
Your mother sits in the waiting

151
00:06:04,890 --> 00:06:06,273
room of an eye clinic.

152
00:06:07,490 --> 00:06:08,810
In the first future,

153
00:06:08,850 --> 00:06:10,490
the room of geniuses has found

154
00:06:10,530 --> 00:06:12,010
hundreds of promising leads

155
00:06:12,050 --> 00:06:14,050
for her kind of blindness.

156
00:06:14,090 --> 00:06:15,810
They're sitting in a queue.

157
00:06:15,850 --> 00:06:18,250
Trials run the way they always have.

158
00:06:18,290 --> 00:06:19,450
The few treatments that make

159
00:06:19,490 --> 00:06:21,077
it through are expensive,

160
00:06:21,117 --> 00:06:23,290
and reach rich cities first.

161
00:06:23,330 --> 00:06:24,650
Nothing collapsed.

162
00:06:24,690 --> 00:06:26,130
We just got a small fraction

163
00:06:26,170 --> 00:06:27,850
of what was possible.

164
00:06:27,890 --> 00:06:28,890
She waits.

165
00:06:29,890 --> 00:06:32,770
In the second future, it's the same clinic.

166
00:06:32,810 --> 00:06:34,170
The same chair.

167
00:06:34,210 --> 00:06:37,610
The drop she takes was a lead in 2026.

168
00:06:37,650 --> 00:06:39,370
Robot labs tested thousands

169
00:06:39,410 --> 00:06:41,370
of versions in a few months.

170
00:06:41,410 --> 00:06:43,290
A trial that once took a decade

171
00:06:43,330 --> 00:06:45,210
took a few careful years,

172
00:06:45,250 --> 00:06:48,010
with real patients and every safety check.

173
00:06:48,050 --> 00:06:50,810
It costs about what an eye drop costs.

174
00:06:50,850 --> 00:06:52,890
She reads the small print on the wall,

175
00:06:52,930 --> 00:06:55,340
out loud, just because she can.

176
00:06:55,790 --> 00:06:57,350
In ten, fifteen years' time,

177
00:06:57,390 --> 00:06:59,870
we'll be in a kind of new golden era of discovery.

178
00:07:00,270 --> 00:07:02,630
I don't know which future we'll get.

179
00:07:02,670 --> 00:07:03,910
Nobody does.

180
00:07:03,950 --> 00:07:06,630
But the second one takes three things.

181
00:07:06,670 --> 00:07:08,030
None of them is magic,

182
00:07:08,070 --> 00:07:10,707
and all three have already worked somewhere.

183
00:07:11,630 --> 00:07:15,240
First: labs that move as fast as ideas.

184
00:07:15,870 --> 00:07:19,960
In 2023, a lab at Berkeley ran itself.

185
00:07:20,030 --> 00:07:22,550
Robots mixed powders, heated them,

186
00:07:22,590 --> 00:07:23,750
measured the result,

187
00:07:23,790 --> 00:07:26,040
and an AI chose the next try.

188
00:07:26,430 --> 00:07:27,990
In a little over two weeks,

189
00:07:28,030 --> 00:07:29,590
it made dozens of materials

190
00:07:29,630 --> 00:07:31,160
it had never made before.

191
00:07:31,630 --> 00:07:33,430
Other scientists later questioned

192
00:07:33,470 --> 00:07:35,480
how many were truly new.

193
00:07:35,550 --> 00:07:37,110
That debate is healthy,

194
00:07:37,150 --> 00:07:38,630
and robots still struggle

195
00:07:38,670 --> 00:07:40,440
with delicate biology.

196
00:07:40,510 --> 00:07:42,550
But the idea is spreading:

197
00:07:42,590 --> 00:07:44,390
labs that work through the night,

198
00:07:44,430 --> 00:07:45,990
so a thousand ideas don't

199
00:07:46,030 --> 00:07:47,880
wait for one pair of hands.

200
00:07:48,750 --> 00:07:50,670
Second: trials that move

201
00:07:50,710 --> 00:07:52,390
at the speed of evidence,

202
00:07:52,430 --> 00:07:54,950
without cutting corners on safety.

203
00:07:54,990 --> 00:07:56,630
We've done this before.

204
00:07:56,670 --> 00:07:58,150
In March 2020,

205
00:07:58,190 --> 00:08:00,550
British doctors set up one trial across

206
00:08:00,590 --> 00:08:02,230
many hospitals, testing

207
00:08:02,270 --> 00:08:04,230
several treatments at once.

208
00:08:04,270 --> 00:08:06,070
Within about a hundred days,

209
00:08:06,110 --> 00:08:09,270
it showed that a cheap steroid saved lives.

210
00:08:09,310 --> 00:08:11,617
Among the sickest patients on ventilators,

211
00:08:11,657 --> 00:08:14,150
it cut deaths by about a third.

212
00:08:14,190 --> 00:08:17,030
The vaccines that year worked the same way.

213
00:08:17,070 --> 00:08:18,870
They didn't skip the steps.

214
00:08:18,910 --> 00:08:20,710
They ran them side by side,

215
00:08:20,750 --> 00:08:21,750
with the money committed

216
00:08:21,790 --> 00:08:23,430
up front and regulators

217
00:08:23,470 --> 00:08:25,750
reading the data as it arrived.

218
00:08:25,790 --> 00:08:27,750
That was an emergency.

219
00:08:27,790 --> 00:08:30,630
The work now is to make that speed normal,

220
00:08:30,670 --> 00:08:33,110
for diseases that kill quietly.

221
00:08:33,150 --> 00:08:34,790
Health agencies and governments

222
00:08:34,830 --> 00:08:36,790
hold that key.

223
00:08:36,830 --> 00:08:39,920
Third: geniuses for everyone.

224
00:08:40,110 --> 00:08:41,830
When Google DeepMind solved

225
00:08:41,870 --> 00:08:43,270
the shape of proteins,

226
00:08:43,310 --> 00:08:45,240
it gave the results away.

227
00:08:45,770 --> 00:08:47,150
We just fold everything

228
00:08:47,190 --> 00:08:48,950
and then give it to everyone in the world.

229
00:08:48,990 --> 00:08:51,590
Who knows how many discoveries will be made from that?

230
00:08:51,990 --> 00:08:53,630
Today, more than three

231
00:08:53,670 --> 00:08:55,710
million researchers use it.

232
00:08:55,750 --> 00:08:58,270
That's what open results can do.

233
00:08:58,310 --> 00:08:59,310
But a market points

234
00:08:59,350 --> 00:09:01,670
its geniuses at what pays.

235
00:09:01,710 --> 00:09:04,430
Diseases of the poor pay the least.

236
00:09:04,470 --> 00:09:06,710
Even Hassabis says the industry isn't

237
00:09:06,750 --> 00:09:09,030
doing enough of this kind of work.

238
00:09:09,070 --> 00:09:10,630
So who decides what a country

239
00:09:10,670 --> 00:09:12,590
of geniuses works on?

240
00:09:12,630 --> 00:09:15,150
Right now, a handful of companies.

241
00:09:15,190 --> 00:09:17,520
They don't have to be the only ones.

242
00:09:17,590 --> 00:09:19,150
Governments can buy computing

243
00:09:19,190 --> 00:09:20,830
time for public science,

244
00:09:20,870 --> 00:09:21,870
the way they once built

245
00:09:21,910 --> 00:09:24,350
telescopes and particle colliders.

246
00:09:24,390 --> 00:09:25,950
Funders can pay for results

247
00:09:25,990 --> 00:09:27,870
that are shared with everyone.

248
00:09:27,910 --> 00:09:30,030
And none of it happens on its own.

249
00:09:30,070 --> 00:09:31,790
The labs, the governments,

250
00:09:31,830 --> 00:09:33,070
and the voters who choose

251
00:09:33,110 --> 00:09:34,350
those governments all

252
00:09:34,390 --> 00:09:36,240
have a hand on that door.

253
00:09:37,790 --> 00:09:39,510
You already carry a thousand

254
00:09:39,550 --> 00:09:41,510
PhDs in your pocket.

255
00:09:41,550 --> 00:09:43,750
Ask them better questions.

256
00:09:43,790 --> 00:09:44,550
What's known about

257
00:09:44,590 --> 00:09:46,390
the illness in your family.

258
00:09:46,430 --> 00:09:47,990
What's being tested.

259
00:09:48,030 --> 00:09:49,510
What it would take.

260
00:09:49,550 --> 00:09:51,390
If you work in science, health,

261
00:09:51,430 --> 00:09:54,270
or government, you're standing at that door.

262
00:09:54,310 --> 00:09:55,670
Hold it open.

263
00:09:55,710 --> 00:09:57,270
And the next time someone tells

264
00:09:57,310 --> 00:09:59,510
you AI will cure everything,

265
00:09:59,550 --> 00:10:01,350
ask one question:

266
00:10:01,390 --> 00:10:04,093
are we building the door, or just the room?

267
00:10:05,310 --> 00:10:07,790
For all of history, progress was limited

268
00:10:07,830 --> 00:10:10,230
by how many brilliant minds we had.

269
00:10:10,270 --> 00:10:12,070
That limit is ending.

270
00:10:12,110 --> 00:10:15,640
What matters now is what we ask them.

271
00:10:15,723 --> 00:10:17,750
So here's my question for you.

272
00:10:17,790 --> 00:10:19,110
If a million geniuses

273
00:10:19,150 --> 00:10:20,550
worked for you tomorrow,

274
00:10:20,590 --> 00:10:22,600
what would you ask them first?

